pipewire-control-center: what changed in each AUR release, newest first.
Every change in full: https://github.com/knightinfected/PipeWireController/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md

0.6.2-1 (2026-10-29)
  The Equalizer, Devices and Patchbay redesigned, Preferences with start at
  login, a balance and a volume for every channel, and 17 bug fixes.

  - Equalizer: drag the curve and hear it as you drag, then compare the
    edit with the saved curve, or with no EQ, at matched loudness.
  - Devices: every device in one list, with the selected one beside it:
    what plays into it, where it goes, and its settings.
  - Patchbay: connect whole devices or channel ranges in one go, arrange
    the graph by signal flow, search and zoom. Video is hidden by default.
  - A balance and a volume for each channel on every output, input and app.
  - Preferences (the gear at the bottom of the sidebar, or Ctrl+,): start
    at login, start minimized in the background, light or dark.
  - The window goes down to 360 pixels wide, for tiling window managers.
  - Fixed, among others: saving an equalizer sent the apps playing through
    it to the speakers; a sound card showed 100 % after a profile switch;
    the Surround speaker test could play on the wrong speakers.

  When you upgrade:
  - Advanced settings are now shown by default, and the sidebar's Advanced
    switch is gone. To hide them, use Preferences → General.
  - Your equalizers keep working as they are. The first edit moves one to
    the new live engine; if you then go back to 0.6.1, its low cut, high
    cut and notch bands turn into plain bells.
  - Start at login uses your desktop's autostart. Hyprland and Sway don't
    run it unless they were started with uwsm (or dex runs it), so
    Preferences → Startup shows the line to add to your config instead.

0.6.1-1 (2026-10-08)
  A .deb for Debian 13 and Ubuntu 26.04, channel maps for any device, and
  fixes for virtual surround that came out far too loud and filter chains
  that would not start.

0.6.0-1 (2026-08-26)
  The dashboard is rebuilt: two views instead of five tabs, pinned
  favourites, and the app now ships its own icons.
